---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

/* Single-sourced so the landing page can quote the entry price without
 * risking drift. See src/data/pricing.mjs. */
import { startupTier, tiers } from '../data/pricing.mjs';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Pricing"
  description={`The complete SDK is free under AGPLv3. Commercial production rights for proprietary products start at ${startupTier.price} ${startupTier.period}.`}
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <div class="container measure">
      <p class="label">Pricing</p>
      <h1>Free under AGPLv3. Priced when you need it closed.</h1>
      {/*
        The positive fact first. The lead opened on three negatives — no trial,
        no feature gate, no usage metering — which are answers to questions the
        reader has not asked yet. What they arrived for is what the free thing
        is and when it stops being free, so those are the first and last
        sentences and the denials sit between them.
      */}
      <p class="lead">
        The complete SDK is available under AGPLv3. No trial, no feature gate, no usage metering.
        Buy a commercial license when your product cannot meet the AGPLv3 obligations.
      </p>
      {/*
        One action. The secondary button read "Understand AGPLv3 use" and
        pointed at /licensing, and the AGPLv3 tier card one screen below
        carries a link with the same label to the same route. Stripe's pricing
        hero carries one call to action; Linear and Sentry each carry one and
        let the tier cards carry the rest.
      */}
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="cta-primary" href="https://docs.open-e2ee.dev/start/quickstart">
          <span class="oe-button">Build your first encrypted message</span>
          <span class="cta-sublabel">two clients · no account · no server</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="band">
    <div class="container">
      <ul class="tiers">
        {tiers.map((tier) => (
          <li class="tier">
            <div class="tier-name">
              <h2>{tier.name}</h2>
              <p class="tier-price">
                {tier.price}
                {tier.period && <span>{tier.period}</span>}
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="tier-body">
              <p>{tier.body}</p>
              <p class="muted">{tier.detail}</p>
              <p class="text-links">
                <a href={tier.cta.href}>{tier.cta.label}</a>
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      {/*
        Which instrument applies to which tier was stated here in a 29-word
        parenthetical, again in "How buying works" below, and a third time on
        /licensing. It is a process step, so "How buying works" owns it and
        this line owns only the precedence rule. "Not this page" went with it:
        naming what does not govern adds nothing to naming what does.
      */}
      <p class="muted measure">
        Taxes may apply. Prices are the annual list prices for the scope named above. Startup
        renews annually until you cancel before the renewal date. Your{' '}
        <a href="/legal/terms">agreement</a> controls the grant, covered products, support,
        renewal, and post-expiration rights.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/*
    "What a breach looks like afterwards" stood here, and it is gone rather
    than moved. It asserted that "HIPAA, SOC 2, GDPR, and the rest are
    satisfied by transport security and encryption at rest", which is the
    opposite of what docs/positioning.md §5 and /blog/tls-is-not-end-to-end-encryption
    both say: TLS plus disk encryption does not establish compliance with a
    complete framework. It escaped `audit-build.mjs` because those patterns
    match the word "compliant", which the sentence never used.

    The argument it was making is the blog post's, made better: that post
    links Cal. Civ. Code §1798.82, the FTC Safeguards Rule and GDPR Art.
    34(3)(a), where this band cited two of them and linked neither. So the
    band goes and the closing row gains the link to it, which this page did
    not have at all.

    This band takes the surface the deleted one had, because the two plain
    bands it left adjacent are what the alternation rule forbids.
  */}
  <section class="band band-surface">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="band-head">
        <h2>How buying works</h2>
      </div>
      <dl class="definition-rows">
        <div>
          <dt>Startup</dt>
          <dd>
            Sign in, name the legal entity and the covered product, accept the commercial terms,
            and complete annual checkout. Checkout records the terms version you accepted, and that
            version governs your license through renewals.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Growth and Enterprise</dt>
          <dd>
            Portfolio rights, redistribution, integration support, security review, and service
            levels need scoping first. A short meeting, then a signed order form.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Evaluating</dt>
          <dd>
            Build the integration under AGPLv3 first. A commercial license changes what you may
            ship. The code is the same build.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p class="text-links">
        <a href="/legal/terms">Review Startup terms</a>
        <a href="mailto:licensing@open-e2ee.dev?subject=Commercial%20licensing%20question">
          Ask a licensing question
        </a>
        <a href="/blog/tls-is-not-end-to-end-encryption/">What a relay breach exposes</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
